    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **276 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change makes telephone numbers stored as digits only, returns the stored value in the `telephone` field, and normalizes before the uniqueness check. Three coordinated edits:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 relaxed the `OwnerFieldsDto` telephone pattern from `^[0-9]*$` to `^[0-9 ()-]*$`. This was necessary because the request DTO is bean-validated (`@Valid`) *before* any normalization runs, so punctuated input like `(613) 555-0100` would otherwise be rejected as a 400 before it could be stripped. The `Owner` entity's `^[0-9]{10}$` constraint still runs on persist, so the *stored* value is guaranteed to be exactly 10 digits.\n\n2. **`.../rest/function/owner/Telephone.java`** (new) \u2014 a small package-private helper, `Telephone.normalize(...)`, that strips spaces, dashes and parentheses (mirroring the existing `DuplicateKey` helper style).\n\n3. **`BuildOwner.java` (POST)** and **`ApplyOwner.java` (PUT)** \u2014 normalize the telephone before setting it on the entity. In the POST pipeline `build \u2192 ensureUnique \u2192 \u2026 \u2192 save`, normalizing in `BuildOwner` means both the uniqueness check (`EnsureOwnerUnique`) and storage see the digits-only value, so `(613) 555-0100` and `6135550100` collide (409) and the stored/returned value is `6135550100`.\n\nI left `DuplicateKey` untouched since it's shared with the email-uniqueness check; because normalization happens upstream in `BuildOwner`, the existing comparison logic works unchanged.\n\nBoth `Cp11Tests` cases (`coreStoresDigitsOnly`, `functionalityNormalisedDuplicateRejected`) now pass, and no existing test regressed.",
